n  has also calculated that he's looked at  and he says he knows how tough the  ukrainians are he certainly understands  that better than we do but he's put that  into his calculation he knows that's  protracted and it'll have  marginal impact initially and maybe  long-term impact for certain and he's  he's accepted if he conducts the  full-out invasion  the cost of all of that as he has  accepted the cost of what he believes is  a tough sanction regime and you know  about the sanctions  he's he's experienced a history of it  they seem to diminish over time and the  country also gets used to them over time  so that's another factor  now the sanctions that we are talking  about imposing i'm not suggesting that  we don't do them i'm all in favor of  them i'm all in favor of hurting putin  significantly and taking down his  central banks and foreign investment  and also i believe dealing with his  industry sectors and certainly the  people involved i agree with all of it  he should pay a price it's just that i  don't believe sanctions are necessarily  the deterrents that people think they  are i think they serve a purpose in  dramatizing the scale of the  international violation that putin has  committed here and you get others to  contribute in that sanction regime and  then and very importantly  it's about punishment and retribution  for the scale of that violation those  things have to be done where it's a  moral imperative to do things like that  but don't kid ourselves to think that  somehow this is going to deter putin  from the objectives that he feels he  intends to achieve here in ukraine well  we know the white house has said that  diplomacy and deterrence were there are  two avenues for preventing this um it  seems that at least at this point  diplomacy has failed although the white  house says they're still open to any  potential diplomatic avenue the  deterrence uh you're saying is not going  to be  useful in terms of stopping the activity  that they're undertaking and who knows  how much further they are determined to  go just yeah go ahead one more point  martha  the biden administration threatened  serious sanctions  after an invasion and they used that  basic promise  to deter that invasion  we now have an invasion  so that strategic policy imperative  has failed that's just the simple facts  of it which i would point out you have  said all along uh on that even a strong  voice saying that nothing would deter  them unless it happened before  they went in and obviously they've  determined that  it's not going to be that punishing and  it's worth the gamble it's worth going  in and as you say these sanctions tend  to deter over time  or diminish over time because they  figure out ways to get around it and  countries get a little bit weaker on  getting stuff in there and wanting to  have that trade so we've seen that  happen over history as well general  thank you so much general jack keane who  will be with us throughout the course of  uh covering this story i think for some  time general thank you very much always  good to have you with us i'm steve ducey  i'm brian kilmeade and i'm ainslie  earhart and click here to subscribe to  the fox news youtube page to catch our  hottest interviews and most compelling  analysis

